---
title: wire
summary: The transportable mark. A class declares how its instances cross an island boundary, with static wire = import.meta.og.wire({ encode, decode }). One strict contract. The macro mints the codec key. Thus there is nothing to import or leak.
related:
  - data-state/state-context
  - reference/constraints
  - macros/overview
---

A prop crosses a **serialization** boundary between islands, not a reference. A plain object copies correctly. A live class instance (a store, an orchestrator) does not. A class opts in when it declares **how it travels**:

```ts title=cart.svelte.ts
export class Cart {
  items = $state<string[]>([]);
  add(item: string) { this.items.push(item); }

  static wire = import.meta.og.wire({
    encode: (c: Cart) => $state.snapshot(c.items),
    decode: (items: string[]) => Object.assign(new Cart(), { items })
  });
}
```

`encode` says what leaves. `decode` says how to rebuild. Both are yours. Thus nothing is hidden. Now a `Cart` instance can be given to islands as a prop. Each island that holds it shares **one live object**. `$state` inside is reactive across all of them.

## Why a call, not a key

You could expect `static [import.meta.og.wire]` as a computed key. The grammar of TypeScript forbids it. A computed class key must be a plain dotted name, and `import.meta.og.wire` is meta-syntax. Thus it can never typecheck. The call form is the strict, typechecked equivalent. The macro **consumes the whole member**, and mints the real symbol key in the compiled output.

```ts
static [Symbol.for('ogygia.wire')] = { encode, decode };
```

The key never exists in your source. Nothing to import, nothing to alias, nothing to leak. The name of the static is convention (`wire`). The call is the mark.

## One contract, strictly enforced

`wire` is legal in exactly one position: a **static member named `wire`**, initialized with the call, inside a class body, with exactly one argument, the codec. Each other use is a build error that names the file, the line, and the fix:

- a bare value (`const k = import.meta.og.wire`)
- a call outside a static member (an object property, a spread, an argument)
- a member with a wrong name, or a non-static member
- a call with no argument, or with more than one argument

Nothing is inferred from the class shape. You always say what leaves and how it comes back. Thus nothing breaks silently.

## Continuity across navigation

The codec accepts two optional fields for **session-lifetime** state:

```ts
static wire = import.meta.og.wire({
  encode, decode,
  id: 'session-cart',                       // promote to a tab-scoped singleton
  merge: (live, fresh) => {                  // reconcile on navigation
    live.serverStamp = fresh.serverStamp;    // pull server truth in; user edits win
  }
});
```

A name on the codec (`id`) makes the instance survive SPA navigations. The same live object follows the visitor across pages, tab-scoped. `merge` reconciles when the server snapshot of a new page arrives: apply what must carry over into the live instance. By default, the live one wins (a cart in the middle of an edit beats a re-read).

Liveness is **identity**, not the codec. Each instance mints one wire id. The browser rebuilds it one time, and memoizes by that id. Thus each copy of the prop joins again into the same object. The **server never memoizes**: each request, and each deferred-island render, decodes fresh. Thus the state of one visitor cannot render into the HTML of another.

See [state & context](/docs/data-state/state-context) for the full picture: share one instance across islands, and provide it to a subtree with `<Provide>` or a drop-in `setContext`.
